(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-1-nervous-system-sensory-quiz"]={"title":"Nervous System: Intro and Sensory — Physiology Exam 1","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which type of sensory receptor detects deformation of tissue?","choices":["Chemoreceptors","Thermoreceptors","Mechanoreceptors","Nociceptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mechanoreceptors detect mechanical deformation, distinct from thermoreceptors (temperature change), nociceptors (tissue damage/pain), electromagnetic receptors (light), and chemoreceptors (taste, smell, CO2, O2). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the \"labeled line principle\" of sensory perception?","choices":["Only pain receptors follow this principle, while all other receptor types transmit multiple different sensory modalities along a single shared pathway","Sensory information from every receptor type converges onto a single shared thalamic relay before random distribution to the cortex","All receptors respond equally to every type of stimulus, with modality determined only by the frequency of firing rather than which fibers are activated","Each receptor responds to a limited range of stimuli and has a direct line to the brain, giving specificity of nerve fibers for transmitting only one modality of sensation"],"answer":3,"exp":"The labeled-line principle is the concept that each receptor responds to a limited range of stimuli and has a direct line to the brain, giving specificity of nerve fibers for transmitting only one modality of sensation. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Why does a Pacinian corpuscle generate a receptor (electrotonic) potential rather than an action potential directly at the receptor?","choices":["The receptor membrane lacks any mechanically-gated ion channels","Pacinian corpuscles depolarize but never repolarize afterward","There are no voltage-gated Na+ channels except in the axon","Receptor potentials are functionally identical to action potentials"],"answer":2,"exp":"Mechanical distortion of a Pacinian corpuscle increases Na+ conductance, generating an electrotonic receptor potential; an action potential only occurs once this signal reaches the axon, where voltage-gated Na+ channels are present. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"How does increased stimulus intensity affect action potential frequency in a sensory neuron?","choices":["Increased stimulus intensity causes an increased receptor potential, which increases AP frequency","Increased stimulus intensity increases the amplitude of individual action potentials rather than their frequency","Increased stimulus intensity has no effect on AP frequency, only on the duration of the receptor potential","Increased stimulus intensity decreases AP frequency by triggering rapid receptor desensitization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Action potentials occur once the receptor potential rises above threshold; increased stimulus intensity causes an increased receptor potential, which in turn increases the frequency of action potentials generated. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is receptor adaptation, and how does the Pacinian corpuscle mechanism work?","choices":["The gradual decrease in receptor response to a sustained stimulus; in the Pacinian corpuscle, fluid redistribution decreases the distorting force over time","Adaptation means the receptor becomes progressively more sensitive to a sustained stimulus; in the Pacinian corpuscle, fluid accumulation increases the distorting force over time","Receptors always fire at a constant, unchanging rate regardless of stimulus duration, since fluid within the Pacinian corpuscle cannot redistribute","Adaptation only occurs in photoreceptors and chemoreceptors, never in mechanoreceptors like the Pacinian corpuscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Receptors adapt (respond less) to a sustained stimulus; in the Pacinian corpuscle, fluid redistribution within the corpuscle decreases the distorting force on the nerve ending over time, while photoreceptors adapt via changes in the amount of light-sensitive chemicals. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are the two mechanisms by which gradations in sensory signal intensity are achieved?","choices":["Only changes in receptor potential amplitude, since spatial and temporal summation do not occur outside the central nervous system","Only changes in axon diameter, which alter conduction velocity but not the perceived intensity of a sensory signal","Spatial summation (increasing the number of fibers stimulated) and temporal summation (increasing the firing rate in a given number of fibers)","Adaptation and facilitation only, without any contribution from either the number or firing rate of stimulated fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gradations in signal intensity are achieved by spatial summation (increasing the number of fibers stimulated) and temporal summation (increasing the rate of firing in a given number of fibers). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What are neuronal pools?","choices":["Groups of neurons with special characteristics of organization, comprising many different types of neuronal circuits (converging, diverging, reverberating, inhibitory)","Groups of neurons found only in the spinal cord gray matter that mediate simple monosynaptic reflexes and nothing more complex than that","A specialized type of glial cell that provides structural and metabolic support to surrounding groups of neurons within the brain","Isolated single neurons with no synaptic connections to any other neuron, functioning entirely independently of neuronal circuits"],"answer":0,"exp":"Neuronal pools are groups of neurons with special characteristics of organization, comprising many different types of neuronal circuits, including converging, diverging, reverberating, and inhibitory circuits. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is an example of the \"amplifying\" type of divergence in neuronal pathways?","choices":["A single sensory neuron branching to synapse on only two or three second-order neurons","Multiple sensory inputs converging onto a single interneuron in the dorsal horn","Reciprocal inhibition of an antagonist muscle pair during a simple stretch reflex","A single pyramidal cell in the motor cortex stimulating several hundred muscle fibers"],"answer":3,"exp":"An amplifying type of divergence is illustrated by a single pyramidal cell in the motor cortex that can stimulate several hundred muscle fibers, spreading a single signal to many targets. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What does convergence of multiple input fibers onto a single neuron provide?","choices":["Faster conduction velocity only, with no change to the pattern or timing of incoming signal integration","Spatial summation, allowing correlation, summation, and sorting of information from multiple sources","Reduction of all incoming signals to a single averaged output, discarding information from individual sources","Only inhibitory effects, canceling out excitatory input from every converging presynaptic fiber"],"answer":1,"exp":"Convergence, where multiple terminals from different incoming fibers terminate on the same neuron, provides spatial summation, allowing the neuron to correlate, summate, and sort information arriving from multiple sources. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is the function of the reciprocal inhibition circuit?","choices":["To increase synaptic delay between the cord and brainstem reflex centers","To control antagonistic pairs of muscles and prevent over-activity in the brain","To amplify excitatory signals equally to both agonist and antagonist muscles","To generate spontaneous action potentials independent of any presynaptic input"],"answer":1,"exp":"The inhibitory (reciprocal inhibition) circuit is important for controlling all antagonistic pairs of muscles and for preventing over-activity in the brain. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What causes a reverberatory (oscillatory) circuit to produce a prolonged output after a brief single input stimulus, and what eventually stops it?","choices":["Positive feedback within the circuit re-excites the input; fatigue of synaptic junctions causes sudden cessation","Output stops only when the presynaptic terminal is permanently destroyed by excitotoxic calcium overload","Negative feedback within the circuit immediately dampens the signal, ending output within milliseconds of the stimulus","Divergence alone accounts for the prolonged output, requiring no feedback mechanism of any kind"],"answer":0,"exp":"A reverberatory circuit produces prolonged output via positive feedback, where the circuit re-excites its own input; the reverberation eventually stops due to fatigue of synaptic junctions (transmitter depletion, receptor inactivation, abnormal ion concentrations). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How does synaptic underactivity versus overactivity affect membrane receptor number?","choices":["Receptor number is fixed and never changes regardless of the level of presynaptic activity at that synapse","Both underactivity and overactivity cause downregulation, reducing sensitivity in either direction","Underactivity leads to upregulation of membrane receptors; overactivity leads to downregulation","Underactivity leads to rapid downregulation; overactivity leads to sustained upregulation of receptors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Underactivity of a synapse leads to upregulation of membrane receptors (increasing sensitivity), while overactivity leads to downregulation of membrane receptors (decreasing sensitivity) — a homeostatic mechanism. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What are the three major levels of CNS function described in this lecture?","choices":["Peripheral, central, and autonomic levels of nervous system organization","Spinal cord level, lower brain level, and higher brain (cortical) level","Cerebrum, cerebellum, and brainstem levels of motor coordination","Sensory, motor, and integrative divisions of cortical function"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three major levels of CNS function are the spinal cord level (contains circuits for walking, withdrawal, posture, organ reflexes), the lower brain level (medulla, pons, mesencephalon, hypothalamus, thalamus, cerebellum, basal ganglia — controls subconscious activities), and the higher brain/cortical level. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"According to this lecture, is the spinal cord merely a conduit for signals passing to and from the brain?","choices":["No — the cord contains its own circuits for walking, withdrawal reflexes, support against gravity, and reflex control of organ function","Yes — the cord merely relays signals passively between peripheral nerves and the brain, with no independent reflex circuitry of its own","The spinal cord's circuits are functionally identical to the cortex, generating voluntary movement without any brainstem input","The spinal cord only contains sensory relay neurons, lacking any motor neurons or interneurons capable of reflex activity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spinal cord is more than just a conduit for signals — it contains its own circuits for walking, withdrawal reflexes, support against gravity, and reflex control of organ function. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What are the three major structural components of a neuron, and which is the \"effector\" portion?","choices":["Dendrite, synapse, soma — the soma is the effector portion (generates the outgoing signal); the synapse is the sensory portion","Soma, nucleus, cytoplasm — the nucleus is the effector portion (extends to the synaptic terminal); the cytoplasm is sensory","Axon, myelin, node of Ranvier — the node of Ranvier is the effector portion; the myelin sheath is the sensory portion","Soma, axon, dendrite — the axon is the effector portion (extends to the synaptic terminal); the dendrite is the sensory portion"],"answer":3,"exp":"A neuron's three major components are the soma (main body), axon (extends to the synaptic terminal, the effector portion), and dendrites (projections from the soma, the sensory portion). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is the chief excitatory transmitter in the CNS, opening cation channels to create an EPSP?","choices":["Glycine","Serotonin","GABA","Glutamate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glutamate is the chief excitatory transmitter in the CNS, opening cation channels (conducting Na+, K+, Ca2+) to create an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is the chief inhibitory transmitter in the adult CNS, opening Cl- channels to create an IPSP?","choices":["Dopamine (a catecholamine)","Glutamate (an amino acid)","GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid)","Acetylcholine (cholinergic)"],"answer":2,"exp":"GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the chief inhibitory transmitter in the adult CNS, opening Cl- channels to hyperpolarize the postsynaptic cell and create an IPSP (though it can be excitatory during development due to differences in Cl- reversal potential). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Why does excitation of a single presynaptic neuron on a dendrite almost never induce an action potential by itself?","choices":["Dendrites cannot receive any synaptic input directly, since all incoming terminals synapse exclusively onto the axon hillock instead","Single synapses always produce large EPSPs (~10-15 mV), but voltage-gated K+ channels on the dendrite immediately shunt the signal to zero","Dendrites have abundant voltage-gated Na+ channels that actively block transmission of any single subthreshold synaptic input","Each terminal produces only a small EPSP (~0.5-1.0 mV); multiple terminals must be excited simultaneously (spatial summation) for the combined EPSP to exceed threshold"],"answer":3,"exp":"Each synaptic terminal on a dendrite produces only a small EPSP (about 0.5-1.0 mV); an action potential requires spatial summation — multiple terminals excited simultaneously — for the combined EPSP to exceed threshold. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What does it mean for a neuron to be \"facilitated\"?","choices":["The summated postsynaptic potential is nearer the threshold for firing than normal but has not yet reached it, making the neuron easier to stimulate with subsequent input","The neuron is permanently inhibited by continuous GABA release, making it impossible to stimulate with any amount of subsequent input","Facilitation only applies to inhibitory neurons, whose postsynaptic potential moves further from threshold with repeated stimulation","The neuron has already fired an action potential and remains in its absolute refractory period, unable to respond to further input"],"answer":0,"exp":"A facilitated neuron has a summated postsynaptic potential nearer the threshold for firing than normal (but not yet at threshold), making it easier to stimulate to firing with subsequent input. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Do dendrites typically transmit action potentials?","choices":["No — dendrites have few voltage-gated Na+ channels and instead transmit signals by electrotonic conduction","Dendrites only conduct true action potentials during periods of maximal spatial summation and high-frequency input","Dendrites and axons are functionally identical, both propagating full-amplitude action potentials via saltatory conduction","Yes, dendrites have abundant voltage-gated Na+ channels, propagating action potentials just as effectively as the axon"],"answer":0,"exp":"Dendrites do not typically transmit action potentials because they have few voltage-gated Na+ channels; instead, they transmit signals by electrotonic conduction, allowing summation of signals from many presynaptic neurons over a large spatial area. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of synaptic facilitation (enhanced responsiveness following repetitive stimulation)?","choices":["Decreased calcium influx at the presynaptic terminal, reducing vesicular release with each successive stimulus","Downregulation of postsynaptic receptors following repeated exposure to the same neurotransmitter over seconds","Progressive depletion of the readily releasable pool of neurotransmitter vesicles at the presynaptic terminal","Build-up of calcium ions in presynaptic terminals, causing more vesicular release of transmitter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Synaptic facilitation is enhanced responsiveness following repetitive stimulation, caused by a build-up of calcium ions in the presynaptic terminal, which increases vesicular release of transmitter. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the functional significance of synaptic fatigue (short-term synaptic depression)?","choices":["It permanently destroys the presynaptic terminal, contributing to the progression of neurodegenerative disease","It only occurs in the peripheral nervous system, playing no role in central circuits like those involved in seizures","It is a protective mechanism against excessive neuronal activity, and may be a mechanism by which epileptic seizures end","It has no protective function, instead worsening excitotoxic damage during periods of sustained neuronal firing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Synaptic fatigue occurs when excitatory synapses are stimulated repetitively at a rapid rate until postsynaptic discharge progressively decreases — a protective mechanism against excessive neuronal activity, possibly contributing to the end of epileptic seizures. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the two main categories of neurotransmitters described, and how do they differ in speed of action?","choices":["Small molecule, rapidly acting transmitters and neuropeptides, slower acting transmitters","Ionotropic transmitters and metabotropic transmitters, distinguished by receptor type rather than speed","Only small molecule transmitters exist in the CNS, since neuropeptides are restricted to the periphery","Fast-acting amines and slow-acting steroids, distinguished by their site of synthesis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Neurotransmitters fall into two main categories: small molecule, rapidly acting transmitters (mediate most acute nervous system responses) and neuropeptides, slower acting transmitters that cause longer-term changes. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"How is glutamate synthesized and released, in contrast to vesicular transmitters?","choices":["It is stored long-term in dense-core vesicles, released only by calcium-triggered exocytosis","It requires no synthesis at all, existing in unlimited free supply within the extracellular space","It is only released via exocytosis of pre-formed vesicles stored at the presynaptic terminal","It is synthesized on demand and does not use vesicles — it diffuses through the membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glutamate (the chief excitatory CNS transmitter, accounting for over 90% of CNS synaptic connections) is synthesized on demand and does not use vesicles for release — it diffuses through the membrane. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What kinds of long-term changes can neuropeptides cause at synapses?","choices":["Only transient changes in synaptic delay lasting a few milliseconds, with no lasting structural effect on the synapse","No lasting changes — they act only transiently and are metabolized within milliseconds like small molecule transmitters","Changes in the number of neuron receptors, long-term opening/closure of ion channels, and changes in the number and size of synapses","Immediate, brief ion channel opening only, lasting a few milliseconds before the channel closes and resets completely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Neuropeptides cause long-term changes including altered numbers of neuron receptors, long-term opening or closure of ion channels, and changes in the number and size of synapses; they act on metabotropic receptors and are often co-released with small molecule transmitters. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which category of somatic sensation includes static position sense and rate of change (proprioception)?","choices":["Nociceptive (specifically fast pain)","A fourth, unnamed sensory category","Mechanoreceptive (specifically proprioceptive)","Thermoreceptive (specifically cold)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Somatic sensations are classified as mechanoreceptive (including tactile — touch, pressure, vibration, tickle, itch — and proprioceptive — static position, rate of change), thermoreceptive (heat/cold), and nociceptive (pain/tissue damage). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What type of nerve fiber carries signals from unmyelinated free nerve endings that detect pain, temperature, and itch?","choices":["Type A-alpha fibers","Only myelinated fibers can detect pain","Type C fibers","Type A-beta fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Unmyelinated free nerve endings that detect pain, temperature, and itch use slowly adapting type C fibers, while myelinated free nerve endings (pain, temperature) use type A-delta fibers. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What are the four classes of sensory nerve fibers mentioned that transmit receptor information to the brain?","choices":["Alpha, beta, gamma, delta","Type I, II, III, IV","Fast, medium, slow, very slow","A, B, C, D"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sensory nerve fibers are classified as alpha, beta, gamma, and delta types, transmitting receptor information to the brain at different conduction velocities. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the relationship between stimulus strength and receptor potential allow a wide response range?","choices":["Receptor potential rises linearly with stimulus strength, so discrimination ability is identical at every intensity level","Small changes in stimulus strength can be discerned at low intensity, while only large changes can be discerned at high intensity","Large changes can be discerned at low intensity, while only small changes can be discerned at high intensity","Only small changes can ever be discerned, regardless of baseline intensity, due to a fixed receptor threshold"],"answer":1,"exp":"Small changes in stimulus strength can be discerned when baseline intensity is low, while only large changes can be discerned when intensity is already high — this logarithmic-like relationship allows receptors to function over a wide range of stimulus intensities. — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are the three broad divisions of the nervous system described in the overview?","choices":["Cortical division, subcortical division, and spinal division","Somatic division, visceral division, and enteric division","Sensory division, integrative division, and motor division","Central division, peripheral division, and autonomic division"],"answer":2,"exp":"The nervous system is organized into a sensory division (tactile, visual, auditory, olfactory), an integrative division (processing information, creating memory), and a motor division (responding to and moving within the environment). — Lecture 3 -Nervous System - Intro and Sensory-2.pptx, Slide 3"}]};
